WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.877
Recording the run like a team would.

2
00:00:02.157 --> 00:00:07.277
A fine-tuning result that only lives in a notebook’s output cells won’t survive a review.

3
00:00:07.557 --> 00:00:26.523
Write down, next to the adapter: the base model id and revision, the dataset version (a hash of the JSONL files is enough), the library versions, every non-default LoRA and training setting, the seed, the hardware, wall-clock time, and both score tables, base and tuned, on the same frozen test set.

4
00:00:26.803 --> 00:00:32.797
Add the three most common failure types with an example of each, and the output checks you added in response.

5
00:00:33.077 --> 00:00:41.547
That is also the skeleton of the model card you’ll publish in week 5, and it’s what lets a colleague reproduce your number or show that it doesn’t hold.

6
00:00:41.827 --> 00:00:53.133
Then hand the result to week 4’s question: on the same test set, with paired comparisons, does this adapter beat a well-prompted frontier model on quality per dollar and per millisecond?

7
00:00:53.413 --> 00:00:59.984
If it doesn’t, the most valuable thing this week produced is the evaluation harness, and that’s still a good outcome.
